        }
     RuleEngine.Evaluate ALSO resets its own cooldown for edge-aware rules
     at rule_engine.go:108-115:
        if !matched && needsFalseEdgeDetection {
            st.LastFiredAt = nil
            st.OnceLatched = false
        }

So both layers have their own cooldown timer AND their own falling-edge
reset. They are NOT redundant — they are stacked, with CooldownFSM acting
as a SECONDARY gate that adds:

  * an hourly fire cap (MaxFiresPerHour: 4) which RuleEngine completely
    lacks;
  * a separate per-pair lock (sync.Mutex) which RuleEngine does not need
    (RuleEngine has its own RWMutex);
  * Suppressed-state metrics (suppressedCount stat) the RuleEngine doesn't
    expose.

There is exactly ONE entry point for streaming alerts in the entire repo
(side_effects_observer:320 → telemetry_alerts:67), and inside that entry
point both systems run in series.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
DUPLICATE_FIRE_RISK
----------------------------------------------------------------------

DUPLICATE FIRE: NO — there is no parallel path. Both gates run inside the
same per-rule loop iteration; the second gate can only suppress, not
re-fire.

But three real semantic bugs DO exist from the duality:

  B1 (state divergence on suppression):
      RuleEngine.Evaluate marks its st.LastFiredAt = now WHEN IT MATCHES
      (rule_engine.go:142). This happens BEFORE CooldownFSM has been
      consulted. If CooldownFSM then suppresses (hourly cap reached, or
      the FSM's cooldown has not lapsed but RuleEngine's HAS — they have
      independent clocks if the FSM was reset by Reset() but RuleEngine's
      cooldown was not), RuleEngine still believes it fired. The next
      RuleEngine cooldown window is off by up to one cycle.

  B2 (no shared state — once-mode latch is in-memory):
      RuleEngine.OnceLatched is in the in-memory map. Pod restart loses
      it. CooldownFSM also resets on restart. If a rule was Once-mode
      latched and the pod restarts, the rule will fire again immediately
      next batch — even though the underlying condition never reset.
      This is the T1 BUG that slice 0002 will fix by persisting latch
      state in alert_rule_state.

  B3 (Reset semantics differ):
      For transition rules:
        - RuleEngine clears st.LastFiredAt AND st.OnceLatched on falling
          edge (rule_engine.go:108-117).
        - CooldownFSM.Reset clears state to Armed AND zeros lastFiredAt,
          but does NOT touch fireCountHour (cooldown.go:107-118 — note
          the deliberate comment "do NOT reset fireCountHour").
      So after a falling edge, the inner gate fully forgets, but the
      outer gate keeps counting hourly fires. A rule that bounces 5 times
      in an hour with falling-edge resets between each will correctly
      fire on rising edges 1-4 then be silently suppressed by the FSM on
      bounce 5 — which is actually the desired storm-protection behavior,
      but is only documented in the FSM source comment and not in any
      ADR or README. Slice 0004 should make this contract explicit.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
RECOMMENDATION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

merge

Rationale:

  * Neither system is dead; deletion of either would lose real behavior.
    - delete_fsm would lose: hourly fire cap, suppressedCount metric,
      Suppressed state visibility.
    - delete_engine_cooldown would lose: integration with OnceLatched
      semantics + the prev-signals "baseline" map that drives changed/
      transition operators (rule_engine.go:76-80, 244-280).

  * Phase-49's locked decisions (D1, D2, slice 0003 max-fires cap, slice
    0009 escalation tier) all want a SINGLE place to ask "should this
    rule fire right now?" — keeping two stacked gates makes those
    decisions ambiguous (which gate enforces max-fires? which one applies
    the escalation grace period?).

  * The merge preserves separation of concerns:
      - RuleEngine continues to own EVALUATION (matched / not-matched
        + once-latched + prev-signals baseline) — this is the rule
        semantics layer.
      - CooldownFSM (renamed to FireGate or AlertGate during slice 0004)
        becomes the SOLE timing layer: cooldown-min, max-fires-per-hour,
        max-fires-per-resolution (slice 0003), escalation grace
        (slice 0009).

  * The merge enables the persistent-latch work in slice 0002:
      - Persist (rule_id, vehicle_id) → latched_at / last_fired_at /
        fire_count_since_reset in the new alert_rule_state table.
      - Both layers read/write to the same row, so divergence (B1) is
        eliminated by construction.

Concrete merge plan for slice 0004:

  1. Move RuleEngine cooldown bookkeeping (LastFiredAt + cooldown
     suppression at rule_engine.go:88-100) into CooldownFSM. RuleEngine
     stops maintaining LastFiredAt and SetLastFired becomes a no-op
     (deprecate, remove after one release).

  2. RuleEngine.Evaluate becomes purely about matching:
        return EvalResult{Triggered: matched, Message: rendered}
     where Triggered means "rule condition is met right now", NOT "fire
     a notification". Caller (TelemetryAlertEvaluator) decides via the
     unified gate.

  3. CooldownFSM owns: cooldown-min duration, max-fires-per-hour,
     max-fires-per-resolution (slice 0003 adds this), escalation grace
     (slice 0009 adds this).

  4. The OnceLatched bit moves to the persistent alert_rule_state row
     (slice 0002), read by RuleEngine on Evaluate, written by the gate
     when it actually fires.

  5. Reset semantics consolidate: ONE Reset(rule, vid) path, called by
     TelemetryAlertEvaluator on falling edge. It clears LastFiredAt +
     latch, leaves fireCountHour and fireCountSinceResolution alone.

